WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.680
Game of Thrones' Night King is a terrifying
villain.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.510
But he may have a connection to one of Westeros'
most noble families: House Stark.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:12.381
In both the books and TV series, there are
plenty of hints indicating that the Night

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.960
King could actually be a Stark.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.470
To understand the connection between House
Stark and the leader of the Others, it's important

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.660
to understand the beginnings of each.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.810
According to The World of Ice and Fire, the
Long Night was a winter that lasted for an

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:30.050
entire generation, thousands of years before
the story we know takes place.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.120
During the Long Night, the Others terrorized
the First Men, the humans who had settled

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.469
in Westeros.

00:00:35.469 --> 00:00:39.399
After much fighting with the Others, one of
the First Men reached an agreement with the

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:43.949
magical beings called the Children of the
Forest, and joined forces with them to repel

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:44.949
the Others.

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:46.199
"Who are you?"

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:50.949
"The First Men called us the Children, but
we were born long before them."

00:00:50.949 --> 00:00:55.420
No one knows for certain who that person was,
but it's hinted that this legendary hero was

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.850
Bran the Builder, the first Stark, who helped
establish the Night's Watch.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.409
Bran would go on to work with the Children
to build the magically-protected Wall to help

00:01:03.409 --> 00:01:05.180
defend against the Others.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.090
Though this battle and the wall's construction
was the first interaction between the Starks

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.920
and the Others, it was far from the last.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.109
Before we go any further, there's an important
distinction to be made between two key figures:

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:20.260
the Night King, and the Night's King.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.829
The Night King is the leader of the White
Walkers in the Game of Thrones TV series.

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:30.170
The Night's King, however, is someone briefly
touched upon in the Song of Ice and Fire books.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.189
The Night's King was a human, and the 13th
Lord Commander of the Night's Watch.

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:41.030
To give you an idea of how long ago this was,
Jon Snow is the 998th Lord Commander.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.460
Or, was.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:43.460
Briefly.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.119
While serving as Commander, the Night's King
fell in love with a mysterious woman, and

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:50.249
together they began making human sacrifices
to the Others.

00:01:50.249 --> 00:01:54.290
After 13 years, with the help of House Stark,
the Night's King and his otherworldly wife

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:55.969
were both defeated.

00:01:55.969 --> 00:02:00.289
The similar names have led some to speculate
that the two may be the same person.

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:04.259
But when asked about the possibility, George
R.R. Martin asserted otherwise, writing,

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:09.340
"As for the Night's King… in the books he
is a legendary figure, akin to Lann the Clever

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:13.560
and Brandon the Builder, and no more likely
to have survived to the present day than they

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.560
have."

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.799
So, that's that.

00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:16.799
They're two different people.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.120
But they also may be deeply connected, by
a lot more than just their similar names.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.629
So, who is the mysterious woman who won the
heart of the old Night's King, turning him

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:28.099
to the Others' side?

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:32.409
It's said that the 13th Lord Commander saw
this woman from atop the Wall.

00:02:32.409 --> 00:02:38.290
In A Storm of Swords, she's said to have "skin
as white as the moon and eyes like blue stars,"

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.099
with skin as cold as ice.

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:44.239
The description heavily implies that she was
one of the Others, sharing their frigid, pale

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:46.790
skin and glowing blue eyes.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:50.849
When Bran tells the story of the Night's King
in A Storm of Swords, he indicates that the

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:53.379
man gave more than just his love to the woman.

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:55.160
He gave his soul as well.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:00.030
The official histories of the Seven Kingdoms,
as represented in The World of Ice and Fire,

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.030
speculate that the Night's King came from
one of the Northern families, of which there

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.379
are many.

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:09.800
But when Old Nan tells the story to Bran,
as Bran recalls in A Storm of Swords, she

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:13.120
puts all other possibilities to rest, saying
without a doubt,

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.689
"He was a Stark, the brother of the man who
brought him down."

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:21.909
The idea that the Night's King from long ago
was a Stark suggests that the modern-day Night

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:26.660
King might have a similar origin story, possibly
being a Stark himself when he was still a

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:27.560
normal man.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:32.840
One of the most heinous taboos in Westeros
is slaying one's own kin.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:37.879
Only in the most extreme of circumstances
would such an act be considered justifiable.

00:03:37.879 --> 00:03:41.870
Brandon the Breaker had no problem killing
his brother the Night's King, but what if

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.209
the Night's King and his mysterious lover
had a child?

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:50.319
As Old Nan confirms to Bran in the novels,
it's entirely possible, with human-White Walker

00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:55.210
couplings resulting in what she calls, quote,
"terrible half-human children".

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:58.700
Slaying the Night's King is one thing, but
an innocent offspring?

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.269
That seems like a line that a Stark wouldn't
cross.

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:05.579
And supposing further that Bran spared this
child's life and then raised them as a Stark,

00:04:05.579 --> 00:04:10.569
it could mean that the Night's King's coupling
with an Other introduced the blood of the

00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:14.690
Others into the Stark line from very early
on in the family's history.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.290
If that theory is true, the family's affinity
for the cold feels pretty natural.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:21.550
As Catelyn Stark put it in the books,

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.200
"The Starks were made for the cold."

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:29.000
The seventh season finale, "The Dragon and
the Wolf," ends on an ominous note, as the

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:32.390
White Walkers break through the Wall and march
into Westeros.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:36.440
During the scene, the camera shows a bird's
eye view of the Army of the Dead marching

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:37.530
forward.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.070
What's interesting about this shot is that
the White Walkers march in a formation that

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:44.220
appears highly reminiscent of the Starks'
house sigil.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.770
The image is flipped, but the resemblance
is uncanny.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.010
There's clearly some sort of connection between
the Stark House and the White Walkers - and

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.120
by extension, the Night King.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:59.800
A common theme in A Song of Ice and Fire is
the burden placed on characters by their ancestors.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.770
Tyrion tries to prove his worth to his father,
but ultimately has to come to terms with his

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:04.820
disapproval.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.851
Jon Snow grows up thinking he's illegitimate,
denied the knowledge that he's heir to the

00:05:08.851 --> 00:05:10.140
Iron Throne.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:15.360
And Daenerys lives in the shadow of her father,
the Mad King Aerys II, and his terrible actions,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.080
which led to the near-destruction of the Targaryen
house.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.160
"I'm not my father."

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.440
"No, Your Grace.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.830
Thank the gods."

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:27.320
If the 13th Lord Commander of the Night's
Watch was a Stark who fell in love with one

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.380
of the Others, it's not a stretch to imagine
that other members of the family could have

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.600
crossed over to their side as well.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.820
We know from the show that the Night King
used to be a man, and considering the history,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.320
it makes the most sense that the Night King
was a Stark.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.240
If it's true, it'll be a real cold reckoning
for the surviving members of House Stark.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.010
It would be especially stressful for Jon Snow.

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:52.530
It's one thing to find out he's related to
his girlfriend, but his mortal enemy too?

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.450
It's almost too much to keep track of.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:58.490
"That's why I read so much, Jon Snow."

